"""Interview questions for Server-Side Fundamentals and MongoDB and NoSQL."""

from app.content._build import qa

INTERVIEW = {
    # Server-Side Fundamentals
    "be-http": [
        qa("What happens when you type a URL into a browser and press Enter?",
           "The browser parses the URL and resolves the host name to an IP address with DNS, checking its caches first. It opens a TCP connection (or QUIC for HTTP/3), performs the TLS handshake for HTTPS, then sends the HTTP request. CDNs, load balancers and reverse proxies route it to application code, which may query caches and databases before the response comes back. The browser then parses the HTML, fetches the CSS, JavaScript and images it references, and renders the page.",
           "Each hop in that answer (DNS, CDN, load balancer, app, database) is also a place to look when a site is slow or down."),
        qa("What is the difference between 401 and 403?",
           "401 Unauthorized really means unauthenticated: the request has no valid credentials, and the response should include a `WWW-Authenticate` header saying how to authenticate. 403 Forbidden means the server knows who you are, but you aren't allowed to do this. Logging in again can fix a 401, never a 403.",
           "This app returns 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Basic` when APP_PASSWORD is set, which is what makes the browser show its login box."),
        qa("What are HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, and why do they matter?",
           "HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one TCP connection and compresses headers, so browsers no longer need many parallel connections. HTTP/3 replaces TCP with QUIC over UDP, which removes TCP's head-of-line blocking when packets are lost and makes connection setup faster, especially on mobile networks. The semantics (methods, status codes, headers) stay the same.",
           "CDNs and load balancers usually speak HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 to browsers for you, so applications get the benefits without code changes."),
    ],
    "be-rest": [
        qa("REST, GraphQL or gRPC: when would you use each?",
           "REST uses resources and HTTP semantics; it's simple, cacheable and universally supported, which suits public APIs. GraphQL lets clients ask for exactly the fields they need in one request, which suits complex frontends with many views, but caching and rate limiting are harder. gRPC sends Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2 for fast, strongly typed calls with streaming, which suits internal service-to-service traffic.",
           "A common setup is REST or GraphQL at the edge for browsers and mobile apps, and gRPC between internal microservices."),
        qa("How do you version an API without breaking clients?",
           "Put the major version in the path (`/v1/`) or a header, and bump it only for breaking changes, such as removing or renaming fields or changing types. Make additive changes (new optional fields, new endpoints) without a new version, and tell clients to ignore fields they don't know. Announce deprecations with dates, for example with a `Sunset` header, and run old and new versions side by side during the migration.",
           "Stripe pins each account to a dated API version, so it can keep evolving its API without breaking existing integrations."),
        qa("How should an API report errors?",
           "Use the right status code plus one consistent JSON body everywhere, such as RFC 9457 Problem Details with `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance`. Include a machine-readable error code clients can branch on, field-level details for validation errors and a request ID for support. Never leak stack traces, SQL or internal hostnames.",
           "Consistent errors let a frontend handle every failure with one function, and request IDs let support find the matching server logs in seconds."),
    ],
    "be-browser-rules": [
        qa("What is CORS, and why can a request work in curl but fail in the browser?",
           "Browsers enforce the same-origin policy: a script on one origin can't read responses from another origin unless the server sends CORS headers allowing it. For non-simple requests, the browser first sends an OPTIONS preflight. curl and server-to-server calls don't enforce CORS at all, so the same request succeeds there. The fix is on the server: allow the specific origins, methods and headers needed, and never combine `*` with credentials.",
           "A frontend on app.example.com calling api.example.com needs CORS; serving both from one origin, or proxying through the dev server as this app does, avoids it entirely."),
        qa("How do you secure session cookies?",
           "Use a long random session ID and keep the session data on the server. Set `HttpOnly` so scripts can't read the cookie, `Secure` so it only travels over HTTPS, and `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` so it isn't sent on most cross-site requests. Give it an expiry, issue a new session ID after login to prevent session fixation, and delete the session on the server at logout.",
           "These attributes turn many XSS and CSRF attacks from account takeovers into harmless failures."),
        qa("How does HTTP caching work, and how do you cache static files safely?",
           "Cache-Control sets the rules: `max-age` for how long a response stays fresh, `public` or `private` for who may store it, `no-store` to forbid storing it and `no-cache` to require checking with the server first. ETag or Last-Modified enable conditional requests that return 304 when nothing changed. For static files, put a content hash in the file name and cache it for a year with `immutable`; a deploy produces new names, so users never get stale code.",
           "Vite builds this app's JavaScript and CSS with hashed names such as index-Cfr4pM-y.js, so they can be cached for a year while index.html stays short-lived."),
    ],
    "be-auth": [
        qa("How should a system store user passwords?",
           "Hash them with a slow, memory-hard algorithm designed for passwords, such as Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt, with a unique random salt per password, and tune the cost so one hash takes tens of milliseconds. Never store plain text, reversible encryption or fast hashes such as MD5 or SHA-256. Compare hashes in constant time, rate-limit login attempts, and rehash with stronger settings when users log in.",
           "When a database leaks, slow salted hashes turn a mass account takeover into an expensive, one-user-at-a-time cracking effort."),
        qa("Sessions or JWTs: which would you choose?",
           "Server-side sessions keep state on the server and send a random ID in a cookie; they're simple, can be revoked instantly and suit browser apps served by your own backend. JWTs are self-contained signed tokens that any service can verify without a lookup, which suits APIs, mobile clients and microservices, but logout and revocation are harder, so you pair short expiry with refresh tokens or a deny-list. Many systems use both: a session cookie at the edge and short-lived tokens between services.",
           "A typical web app keeps a session cookie in the browser, while its API gateway swaps it for a short-lived JWT that internal services verify."),
        qa("How do access tokens and refresh tokens work together?",
           "The access token is short-lived (often 5 to 15 minutes) and sent with every API request, so a stolen one is useful only briefly. The refresh token is long-lived, stored more carefully (for example in an HttpOnly cookie) and only sent to the token endpoint to get new access tokens. Rotating the refresh token on every use, and revoking the whole chain if an old one is reused, detects theft.",
           "This is how OAuth providers keep users signed in for weeks without long-lived bearer tokens travelling on every request."),
    ],
    "be-authz": [
        qa("What is the difference between authentication and authorization?",
           "Authentication verifies identity: a password, passkey, token or certificate proves who the caller is. Authorization decides what that identity may do: which endpoints, actions and objects. Authentication comes first. Failing it returns 401; failing authorization returns 403, or 404 to hide that an object exists.",
           "In FastAPI this maps to layers of dependencies: one resolves the current user, and others check roles and ownership."),
        qa("Explain the OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with PKCE.",
           "The client creates a random code verifier and sends its SHA-256 hash (the code challenge) when it redirects the user to the authorization server. The user signs in and consents, and the server redirects back with a short-lived authorization code and the `state` value, which the client checks to block CSRF. The client exchanges the code plus the original verifier for tokens, so an attacker who intercepts the code can't redeem it.",
           "It's the recommended flow for web, mobile and single-page apps, and it's what 'Sign in with Google' or GitHub uses under the hood."),
        qa("What is IDOR (BOLA), and how do you prevent it?",
           "Insecure direct object reference, or broken object-level authorization, happens when an API trusts an ID from the client without checking that the caller may access that object. Prevent it by checking ownership or permission for every object on the server, ideally by scoping queries to the user (`WHERE id = ? AND owner_id = ?`), and by testing cross-user access. Random IDs make guessing harder but don't fix the bug on their own.",
           "BOLA is number one in the OWASP API Security Top 10 because it's easy to miss in code review and has caused many real data leaks."),
    ],
    "be-security": [
        qa("What is XSS, and how do you prevent it?",
           "Cross-site scripting injects attacker-controlled script into pages that other users view, letting it steal data or act as them. Stored XSS saves the payload (in a comment, say), reflected XSS bounces it off a URL parameter, and DOM XSS happens in client-side code. Prevent it by escaping output for its context by default, avoiding raw-HTML APIs such as innerHTML with untrusted data, sanitizing any HTML you must allow, adding a Content-Security-Policy and marking cookies HttpOnly.",
           "React and Jinja2 escape by default; most XSS bugs appear where developers bypass that, for example with dangerouslySetInnerHTML."),
        qa("What is CSRF, and how do SameSite cookies help?",
           "Cross-site request forgery makes a victim's browser send a state-changing request to a site where they're logged in, such as a hidden form that posts to /transfer; the browser attaches the cookies automatically. Defences: SameSite=Lax or Strict cookies (not sent on cross-site POSTs), a CSRF token checked on every state change, checking the Origin header, and never changing state on GET. APIs that authenticate with an Authorization header instead of cookies aren't vulnerable in the same way.",
           "Django and Rails put CSRF tokens in forms by default, and modern browsers now treat cookies without a SameSite attribute as Lax."),
        qa("What is SSRF, and how do you defend against it?",
           "Server-side request forgery abuses a feature that makes the server fetch a URL, steering it to internal services the attacker can't reach directly, such as cloud metadata endpoints that hand out credentials. Defend with an allowlist of hosts and schemes, resolve DNS and block private, loopback and link-local ranges, connect to the IP you validated (to stop DNS rebinding), re-check or disable redirects, and run the fetcher on a network with no internal access.",
           "SSRF against the AWS metadata service was central to the 2019 Capital One breach, and AWS's IMDSv2 now requires a session token partly because of attacks like it."),
    ],
    "be-concurrency": [
        qa("Threads, processes or async: how do you choose in Python?",
           "Use async for high-concurrency I/O, such as many network or database calls, when async libraries are available. Use threads for I/O with blocking libraries, or to wrap sync code. Use processes (multiprocessing, ProcessPoolExecutor or several server workers) for CPU-bound work, because the GIL stops threads running Python code in parallel. Real servers combine them: several processes, each running an event loop with a thread pool for blocking calls.",
           "`uvicorn --workers 4` is exactly this mix: four processes for the CPU cores, each running an async event loop."),
        qa("What is the GIL, and does it make Python bad for web servers?",
           "The Global Interpreter Lock is a mutex in CPython that lets only one thread run Python bytecode at a time. It's released during I/O and in many C extensions, so I/O-bound servers, which spend most of their time waiting on networks and databases, scale fine with threads or async. CPU-bound work is handled with multiple processes or native libraries. Python 3.13 added an experimental free-threaded build without the GIL.",
           "Instagram has run one of the world's largest Django deployments by scaling out worker processes rather than threads."),
        qa("Your async API gets slow under load, but CPU usage is low. What would you check?",
           "Low CPU with high latency means the server is waiting, not computing. Check for blocking calls inside async code (sync HTTP clients, sync database drivers, time.sleep, large file reads), exhausted connection pools to the database or other services, slow downstream dependencies without timeouts, and lock contention. asyncio's debug mode logs slow callbacks, and tracing shows which dependency the time goes to.",
           "A single `requests.get` inside an `async def` route is one of the most common causes of mysteriously slow FastAPI services."),
    ],
    "be-observability": [
        qa("What are logs, metrics and traces, and when do you use each?",
           "Logs are timestamped event records with rich detail, best for investigating what happened in a specific case. Metrics are numbers aggregated over time (request rate, error rate, latency percentiles, queue depth); they're cheap and drive dashboards and alerts. Traces record the path and timing of a single request through every service and dependency, which shows where latency comes from. Metrics tell you something is wrong, traces show where, and logs explain why.",
           "A typical incident starts with a metric alert (p99 latency is up), moves to traces to find the slow dependency, then to logs filtered by trace or request ID."),
        qa("What is the difference between liveness and readiness probes?",
           "A liveness probe checks whether the process is healthy at all; if it fails, the orchestrator restarts the container. A readiness probe checks whether the instance can serve traffic right now; if it fails, the instance is taken out of load balancing but not restarted. Readiness may check critical dependencies; liveness shouldn't, or a database blip would restart every instance at once.",
           "Kubernetes uses both (plus startup probes for slow starters); Render and most load balancers use one health-check path, such as this app's /api/health."),
        qa("One endpoint is slow in production. How do you find out why?",
           "Confirm the scope with metrics: which endpoint, since when, for all users or some, at which percentile. Use traces to see which part takes the time (a database query, an external API, serialization, waiting for a connection). Then dig in: EXPLAIN the slow query, look for N+1 queries, missing indexes, cold caches, pool exhaustion or a recent deploy. Fix one thing, verify with the same metrics, and add an alert if one was missing.",
           "Interviewers look for a systematic approach (measure, narrow down with data, change one thing, verify) rather than guessing."),
    ],
    # MongoDB and NoSQL
    "mongo-intro": [
        qa("SQL or NoSQL: how do you choose between PostgreSQL and MongoDB?",
           "Start from access patterns and consistency needs. Relational databases suit structured, highly related data, ad-hoc queries, joins and reporting, with strong constraints. Document databases suit aggregates that are read and written together (an order with its items), varied or evolving attributes, and very large scale with built-in sharding. Both now support transactions and JSON, so pick the one whose model matches how the application reads data, and remember that the team's experience matters too.",
           "Many companies use both: PostgreSQL for billing and accounts, MongoDB for catalogs, content or event data."),
        qa("What is an ObjectId, and what does it contain?",
           "An ObjectId is MongoDB's default `_id`: 12 bytes made of a 4-byte Unix timestamp, a 5-byte random value per process and a 3-byte counter. Drivers generate it without coordinating with the server, it's unique in practice, and it sorts roughly by creation time. `getTimestamp()` reads the creation time back.",
           "Because ObjectIds sort roughly by time, `sort({ _id: -1 })` is a cheap way to get the newest documents without a separate createdAt index."),
        qa("What does 'schemaless' really mean in MongoDB?",
           "MongoDB doesn't force every document in a collection to have the same fields, but your application still has a schema in practice, because the code expects certain fields. Good teams make it explicit: validate in the application (with Pydantic, say) and with collection-level `$jsonSchema` validators, and plan migrations for changed fields. The flexibility helps with evolving data and optional attributes; it isn't an excuse for inconsistent data.",
           "A product catalog can store different attributes for shoes and laptops in one collection, while a validator still requires a name, price and SKU on every product."),
    ],
    "mongo-crud": [
        qa("How does MongoDB guarantee atomicity for updates?",
           "Every write to a single document is atomic, including changes to embedded documents and arrays, so no reader ever sees a half-applied `$inc` or `$push`. Operations over several documents, such as `updateMany`, are atomic per document, not as a whole. For all-or-nothing changes across documents, use a transaction, or model the data so the change touches only one document.",
           "A shopping cart stored as one document with an items array can be updated atomically with `$push` and `$inc`, with no transaction needed."),
        qa("Why use $inc instead of reading a value and writing it back?",
           "Read-modify-write in the application creates a race: two requests read the same value and both write back value + 1, losing one increment. `$inc` makes the change on the server atomically, so every concurrent increment is applied. Conditional updates work the same way: `updateOne({ _id, stock: { $gte: 1 } }, { $inc: { stock: -1 } })` decrements stock only if some is left.",
           "View counters, likes and inventory reservations all rely on atomic operators to stay correct under heavy concurrency."),
        qa("What is the difference between updateOne with $set and replaceOne?",
           "`updateOne` with `$set` changes only the named fields and leaves the rest of the document untouched. `replaceOne` swaps the entire document (except `_id`) for the new one, so any field you didn't include disappears. Prefer targeted operator updates: they're smaller, safer under concurrency and don't drop fields that another part of the system added.",
           "Replacing documents with stale copies is a classic bug in which one service silently erases fields written by another."),
    ],
    "mongo-queries": [
        qa("When do you need $elemMatch?",
           "When several conditions must hold for the same array element. A query such as `{ \"reviews.user\": \"ada\", \"reviews.stars\": { $gte: 4 } }` matches if any element has user ada and any element (possibly a different one) has 4 or more stars. `$elemMatch` applies both conditions to a single element. With only one condition, you don't need it.",
           "Filtering orders for 'an item with SKU X and quantity over 10' gives wrong results without $elemMatch, a subtle bug interviewers like to probe."),
        qa("How do you paginate efficiently in MongoDB?",
           "skip and limit are simple, but MongoDB still walks past every skipped document, so deep pages get slow, and results shift when data changes. Range-based (keyset) pagination remembers the last sort key, such as `_id` or `(createdAt, _id)`, and queries `{ _id: { $gt: lastId } }` with a sort and limit on an index, so every page is equally fast. Return the cursor value to the client as an opaque token.",
           "Infinite-scroll feeds and APIs over millions of documents use keyset pagination; skip is fine for small admin lists."),
        qa("How do you prevent NoSQL injection?",
           "If user-controlled JSON goes straight into a filter, an attacker can send operators instead of values, for example `{ \"password\": { \"$ne\": null } }`, which matches any password. Validate input types (a string must be a string) with a schema such as Pydantic, never build filters from raw request bodies, escape user input used in `$regex`, and avoid server-side JavaScript such as `$where`. Treat `$`-prefixed keys from users as invalid.",
           "Login endpoints in Node.js apps were a classic victim when `req.body.password` was dropped directly into `findOne`."),
    ],
    "mongo-modeling": [
        qa("How do you decide whether to embed or reference?",
           "Start from the queries. Embed when data is read together, owned by one parent and bounded in size (one-to-few), which gives single-read access and atomic updates. Reference when the related data is shared, updated independently, large or unbounded (one-to-squillions), or queried on its own. Hybrids are common: reference the full data and embed a small, frequently read subset, such as an author's name.",
           "E-commerce systems embed line items in orders but reference customers and products, copying the price and title at the time of purchase."),
        qa("What are common MongoDB schema design patterns?",
           "Subset: embed only the most-used part (the latest 10 reviews) and keep the rest in another collection. Extended reference: copy a few fields of a referenced document to avoid lookups. Computed: store precomputed totals or counts, updated on write. Bucket: group time-series readings into one document per hour. Outlier: handle rare huge cases, such as a celebrity with millions of followers, separately. Each trades extra write work or duplication for faster reads.",
           "IoT and analytics systems use the bucket pattern (or MongoDB's native time-series collections) to cut document counts and index sizes dramatically."),
        qa("How do you handle schema changes in a document database?",
           "Old and new document shapes can coexist, so you can migrate gradually: add a `schemaVersion` field, make the code read both versions and upgrade documents when they're next written, or run a background migration in batches. Additive changes (new optional fields) are easy; renames and type changes need a plan, often dual writes plus a backfill. Keep validators and application models in step with the migration.",
           "Teams avoid downtime by first deploying code that understands both versions, then backfilling, then removing the old-version code."),
    ],
    "mongo-indexes": [
        qa("Explain the ESR rule for compound indexes.",
           "Order compound index fields as Equality, Sort, Range. Equality fields narrow the index to one contiguous section; the sort field next means entries in that section are already in the requested order, so no in-memory sort is needed; range fields last are filtered within that ordered section. Putting a range field before the sort field forces MongoDB to sort the results in memory.",
           "Getting this order right often turns a 2-second query with an in-memory sort into one that takes a few milliseconds."),
        qa("How do you find and fix a slow MongoDB query?",
           "Find it with the database profiler or the slow-query log (or Atlas's Query Profiler and Performance Advisor). Run `explain(\"executionStats\")` and look for COLLSCAN, an in-memory SORT and a high ratio of documents examined to documents returned. Add or adjust an index (following ESR), project fewer fields, and rewrite unanchored regexes or `$ne` filters that use indexes poorly, then check explain again. Keep an eye on the number of indexes, since each one slows writes.",
           "Atlas's Performance Advisor suggests indexes based on real slow queries, but you should still verify each suggestion with explain."),
        qa("What do indexes cost?",
           "Every insert, update and delete must update every affected index, so write latency and throughput suffer. Indexes take disk space and, to be fast, must fit in RAM alongside the working set. Too many similar indexes also make the query planner's job harder. Keep the indexes that support real queries, merge overlapping ones using prefixes, and use `$indexStats` to find unused ones.",
           "Write-heavy collections such as event logs usually carry only one or two indexes, while read-heavy catalogs can afford more."),
    ],
    "mongo-aggregation": [
        qa("How would you optimise an aggregation pipeline?",
           "Filter and trim early: put `$match` first so it can use an index, and put `$sort` with `$limit` early so MongoDB can use an index or a top-k sort. Project away unneeded fields before expensive stages, avoid `$unwind` on huge arrays when an array operator would do, and index the foreign field of each `$lookup`. Check the plan with `explain()`, and for big jobs precompute results into a summary collection with `$merge`.",
           "Dashboards often run a nightly `$merge` pipeline into a summary collection, so page loads read a few precomputed documents instead of aggregating millions."),
        qa("Does MongoDB support joins, and when should you use $lookup?",
           "Yes: `$lookup` performs a left outer join, including pipeline-based lookups with extra conditions. It's fine for occasional queries, reports and admin views. If a busy code path needs a join on every request, that usually means the model should embed or duplicate some data, because document databases are fastest when one read returns everything. Lookups across shards in a sharded cluster are also more expensive.",
           "An order-history page should read from one orders collection with embedded line items, while a monthly sales report can $lookup customers."),
        qa("How does the aggregation pipeline map to SQL?",
           "$match is WHERE (or HAVING when it comes after $group), $group with accumulators is GROUP BY with aggregate functions, $project or $set is SELECT, $sort is ORDER BY, $limit is LIMIT, $lookup is a LEFT JOIN, and $unwind is like joining against an unnested array. Pipelines are explicit step-by-step transformations, which makes reshaping documents easy but cross-collection joins less natural.",
           "Being able to translate between the two lets you move reports between PostgreSQL and MongoDB, and helps in interviews that ask you to rewrite a SQL query as a pipeline."),
    ],
    "mongo-python": [
        qa("How does connection pooling work in MongoDB drivers?",
           "A MongoClient keeps a pool of connections to each server, hands one out per operation and returns it afterwards, which avoids a new TCP and TLS handshake per request. Create one client per process at startup and reuse it everywhere. Tune `maxPoolSize` (100 by default) and timeouts such as `serverSelectionTimeoutMS`; creating a client per request exhausts the server's connections and is a common production bug.",
           "Serverless functions should create the client outside the handler, so warm invocations reuse its connections."),
        qa("How do you use MongoDB in an async Python web app?",
           "Use an async driver so database waits don't block the event loop: PyMongo's `AsyncMongoClient`, the successor to Motor. Create the client once (for example in FastAPI's lifespan), create indexes at startup and `await` each operation. Calling the synchronous `MongoClient` inside `async def` routes blocks the loop and serialises every request on that worker.",
           "This connects to the Server-Side Fundamentals course: a blocking driver inside async code is a top cause of slow FastAPI services."),
        qa("How would you design a data access layer for MongoDB?",
           "Wrap collection access in repository classes or functions with clear methods (create, get, search) so routes don't build queries directly. Validate inputs with typed models (Pydantic), convert ObjectId and datetime values at the boundary, cap page sizes, escape regex input and translate driver errors (DuplicateKeyError) into domain errors. Queries then live in one place, indexes are easy to match to them, and you can test against a temporary database.",
           "When a query needs a new index or the schema changes, a repository layer means editing one file instead of every route."),
    ],
    "mongo-transactions": [
        qa("Does MongoDB support ACID transactions?",
           "Yes. Single-document operations have always been atomic, and MongoDB supports multi-document ACID transactions with snapshot isolation on replica sets (since 4.0) and sharded clusters (since 4.2). They cost extra latency, have a default 60-second limit and increase contention, so the recommended approach is to model data so most operations touch one document, and use transactions for the genuinely multi-document cases, such as transfers.",
           "A payments ledger can use transactions for transfers while the product catalog never needs one."),
        qa("Explain write concern and read concern.",
           "Write concern controls durability: how many members must confirm a write (`w: 1` or `w: \"majority\"`) and whether it must be in the journal (`j: true`). With `w: 1`, a confirmed write can be rolled back if the primary fails before replicating it. Read concern controls visibility: `local` returns the node's latest data, `majority` returns only data confirmed by a majority (which can't be rolled back), and `linearizable` is the strongest. Stronger settings cost latency.",
           "Order and payment writes use `w: \"majority\"`, while high-volume analytics events may accept `w: 1` for speed."),
        qa("How do you get read-your-writes consistency when reading from secondaries?",
           "Secondaries replicate asynchronously, so a read right after a write can miss it. The options: read from the primary for that user's flows; use a causally consistent session with majority read and write concerns, so reads wait until the secondary has caught up with the session's last write; or route a user's reads to the primary for a short time after they write.",
           "After a user edits their profile, the confirmation page reads from the primary so the user never sees the old value."),
    ],
    "mongo-scale": [
        qa("How do replication and failover work in a MongoDB replica set?",
           "The primary records every write in its oplog, a capped collection of operations; secondaries tail the oplog and apply the operations, usually within milliseconds. Members exchange heartbeats, and if the primary is unreachable for the election timeout (10 seconds by default), an eligible secondary calls an election; the one with the most recent data and a majority of votes becomes primary. Writes confirmed only by the old primary (`w: 1`) can be rolled back, which is why `w: \"majority\"` matters.",
           "Three members across three availability zones keep a cluster writable when any one zone goes down."),
        qa("How do you choose a shard key?",
           "Look for high cardinality (many distinct values), low frequency (no single value dominates) and writes that don't always increase, and make sure the most common queries include the key so they go to one shard. Hashed keys spread writes evenly but lose range locality; ranged and compound keys keep related data together. Since MongoDB 5.0 you can reshard a live collection, but it's expensive, so choosing well up front still matters.",
           "Multi-tenant SaaS apps often shard by `{ tenantId: 1, ... }`, so each tenant's queries hit one shard and tenants are spread across the cluster."),
        qa("What is the difference between replication and sharding?",
           "Replication copies the same data to several servers for high availability, durability and read capacity; every member holds the full data set. Sharding partitions the data so each shard holds a subset, which scales writes and storage beyond one machine. Production sharded clusters use both: each shard is itself a replica set.",
           "You add replicas when you need resilience or more read capacity, and shards when one primary can't keep up with the writes or the data no longer fits."),
    ],
}
